From 685ce87b2aa2689c16b9ebcc89433f7562c02691 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 12:59:47 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 116/116] Input: gpio-keys - do not arm a switch that is
 already released for wakeup on release

A switch whose wakeup-event-action is EV_ACT_DEASSERTED wakes the
system when it is released. If it is released already when the system
suspends, it cannot be released again before it has been asserted, and
the assertion is not a wakeup event: arming it brings nothing.

It can cost the whole sleep. On the OnePlus Pad 4 the cover's hall
switch (TLMM 98, active low, wake on open) is routed to the PDC, which
latches one rising edge on the idle-high line each time the SoC leaves
CX collapse in s2idle, with the supply of the sensor held on. With the
cover open every such sleep ended at the first CX exit, after half a
second; with the switch unarmed they ran to their RTC alarm.

Leave such a switch unarmed. Keys and the other wakeup event actions
are not touched, and a switch that is asserted when the system suspends
(a closed cover) is armed as before. The error path of
gpio_keys_enable_wakeup() now disarms only what was armed, like
gpio_keys_disable_wakeup() does.

Tested on the OnePlus Pad 4 (area-combo-64): with the cover open and
the lid wake left enabled, every suspend-to-idle sleep that reached
CX collapse (98 % of a 150 s sleep) ended by the RTC alarm; the edge
the PDC latches is replayed once at resume without an input event.

Signed-off-by: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/input/keyboard/gpio_keys.c | 15 ++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/input/keyboard/gpio_keys.c b/drivers/input/keyboard/gpio_keys.c
index 129fc4212..5cf141cde 100644
--- a/drivers/input/keyboard/gpio_keys.c
+++ b/drivers/input/keyboard/gpio_keys.c
@@ -946,6 +946,18 @@ gpio_keys_button_enable_wakeup(struct gpio_button_data *bdata)
 {
 	int error;
 
+	/*
+	 * A switch that wakes the system when it is released, and is released
+	 * already, cannot be released again before it has been asserted, which
+	 * is not a wakeup event. Leave it unarmed: wakeup controllers can latch
+	 * an edge on an idle line when the SoC leaves a power collapse, and
+	 * that would end the sleep for nothing.
+	 */
+	if (bdata->button->type == EV_SW &&
+	    bdata->button->wakeup_event_action == EV_ACT_DEASSERTED &&
+	    bdata->gpiod && gpiod_get_value_cansleep(bdata->gpiod) == 0)
+		return 0;
+
 	error = enable_irq_wake(bdata->irq);
 	if (error) {
 		dev_err(bdata->input->dev.parent,
@@ -1025,7 +1037,8 @@ gpio_keys_enable_wakeup(struct gpio_keys_drvdata *ddata)
 err_out:
 	while (i--) {
 		bdata = &ddata->data[i];
-		if (bdata->button->wakeup)
+		if (bdata->button->wakeup &&
+		    irqd_is_wakeup_set(irq_get_irq_data(bdata->irq)))
 			gpio_keys_button_disable_wakeup(bdata);
 		bdata->suspended = false;
 	}
-- 
